# Selecting Single Elements 🎯

## 1. `document.getElementById('id')`:
Fastest way to select an element by its unique ID:
```javascript_not
const header = document.getElementById('main-header');
```

## 2. `document.querySelector('cssSelector')` (Preferred):
Selects the **first** element that matches any valid CSS selector:
```javascript_not
// By tag name
const firstParagraph = document.querySelector('p');

// By class name
const submitBtn = document.querySelector('.btn-primary');

// By complex CSS selector
const activeLink = document.querySelector('nav ul li.active a');
```

# Selecting Multiple Elements 📚

## `document.querySelectorAll('cssSelector')`:
Returns a static `NodeList` containing **all** matching elements on the page:

```javascript_not
const cards = document.querySelectorAll('.card');

console.log(`Found ${cards.length} cards`);

// Iterate through the NodeList using forEach
cards.forEach((card, index) => {
  console.log(`Card ${index + 1}:`, card);
});
```

> ⚠️ **NodeList vs HTMLCollection:** `querySelectorAll` returns a `NodeList` which has built-in `.forEach()`. Older methods like `getElementsByClassName` return an `HTMLCollection` which lacks array methods!

# Modifying HTML Attributes ⚙️

Interact directly with element attributes:

```javascript_not
const link = document.querySelector('a');

// 1. Get an attribute
const currentUrl = link.getAttribute('href');

// 2. Set an attribute
link.setAttribute('href', 'https://github.com');
link.setAttribute('target', '_blank');

// 3. Remove an attribute
link.removeAttribute('title');

// 4. Check if attribute exists
if (link.hasAttribute('href')) {
  console.log("Link is valid");
}
```

# Modifying Styles with `.style` 🎨

Modify inline CSS properties directly:

```javascript_not
const card = document.querySelector('.card');

card.style.backgroundColor = '#f8fafc'; // CamelCase for hyphenated CSS properties!
card.style.padding = '24px';
card.style.display = 'flex';
card.style.borderRadius = '12px';
```

> 💡 **Notice:** CSS properties with hyphens (like `background-color` and `border-radius`) become **camelCase** in JavaScript (`backgroundColor` and `borderRadius`).

# The `classList` API (Best Practice) 🏆

Directly writing inline styles with `.style` mixes presentation into JavaScript. The industry best practice is toggling predefined CSS classes with `classList`!

```javascript_not
const banner = document.querySelector('.banner');

// Add classes
banner.classList.add('active', 'shadow');

// Remove class
banner.classList.remove('hidden');

// Toggle class (adds if absent, removes if present!)
banner.classList.toggle('dark-mode');

// Check if class exists
if (banner.classList.contains('active')) {
  console.log("Banner is currently active");
}
```

# Creating Elements with `createElement` 🏗️

Create new DOM elements dynamically in memory:

```javascript_not
// 1. Create a new <div> element
const newCard = document.createElement('div');

// 2. Configure its classes and content
newCard.classList.add('product-card');
newCard.textContent = "Wireless Bluetooth Headphones";

// 3. The element exists in memory, but is NOT on screen yet!
```

# Inserting Elements into the Document 📌

Attach your in-memory elements to the live DOM tree:

```javascript_not
const container = document.getElementById('card-container');

// append(): Adds element to the end of the container
container.append(newCard);

// prepend(): Adds element to the very beginning of the container
container.prepend(newCard);

// before() / after(): Inserts as sibling before or after container
container.after(newCard);
```

> 💡 **`append` vs `appendChild`:** Modern `.append()` allows appending multiple elements and strings at once; legacy `.appendChild()` only accepts a single Node.

# Removing Elements (`remove`) 🗑️

Delete an element directly with `.remove()`:

```javascript_not
const alertMessage = document.querySelector('.alert');

// Removes the element from the DOM completely!
alertMessage.remove();
```

Or remove child from parent:
```javascript_not
const parentList = document.querySelector('ul');
const firstItem = parentList.firstElementChild;
parentList.removeChild(firstItem);
```

# DOM Traversal: Navigating the Tree 🧭

Navigate between related elements without querying again:

```javascript_not
const currentItem = document.querySelector('.active-item');

// 1. Parent
const parent = currentItem.parentElement;

// 2. Siblings
const nextSibling = currentItem.nextElementSibling;
const prevSibling = currentItem.previousElementSibling;

// 3. Children
const allChildren = parent.children; // HTMLCollection of child elements
const firstChild = parent.firstElementChild;
const lastChild = parent.lastElementChild;
```

# The `.closest()` Method 🎯

The `closest()` method traverses upwards through ancestors until it finds the closest element matching a CSS selector:

```javascript_not
// When user clicks a button deep inside a table row:
button.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  // Finds the parent table row holding this button!
  const row = event.target.closest('tr');
  row.remove();
});
```

Extremely powerful for event handling and component architectures!

# Performance: `DocumentFragment` ⚡

Adding elements to the DOM in a loop causes repeated browser layout recalculations:

```javascript_not
// ❌ SLOW: 100 separate DOM reflows!
for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
  const p = document.createElement('p');
  document.body.append(p);
}

// ✅ FAST: 1 single DOM reflow using DocumentFragment!
const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();

for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
  const p = document.createElement('p');
  p.textContent = `Item ${i}`;
  fragment.append(p); // In memory!
}

document.body.append(fragment); // Single DOM touch!
```

# Real-World: Dynamic Task Creator 📝

```javascript_not
function addTask(taskText) {
  const list = document.getElementById('task-list');

  // Create list item
  const li = document.createElement('li');
  li.classList.add('task-item');

  // Create text span
  const span = document.createElement('span');
  span.textContent = taskText;

  // Create delete button
  const delBtn = document.createElement('button');
  delBtn.textContent = "✕";
  delBtn.addEventListener('click', () => li.remove());

  li.append(span, delBtn);
  list.append(li);
}
```
